(function () {
  var m = {"AIM278-S": {"abstract": "Production agents need more than infrastructure. They need an open source foundation for understanding behavior, evaluating quality, and improving continuously without sacrificing control of their data. In this session, learn how Amazon Bedrock AgentCore and Langfuse combine secure agent infrastructure with open source observability and evaluation, powered by ClickHouse. See how teams capture OpenTelemetry traces, debug trajectories and tool calls, evaluate production quality, turn failures into datasets, and prevent regressions before deployment. We will share a practical architecture for unifying policy and telemetry across AgentCore, EKS, and other runtimes so teams can ship trustworthy agents faster. This presentation is brought to you by ClickHouse, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"AIM281-S": {"abstract": "As generative AI matures into autonomous ecosystems, enterprise marketing demands a new era of collaboration. In this session, discover how Adobe and Amazon co-innovate to deliver next-generation AI capabilities for marketers, business practitioners, and developers. At the center of this revolution is Adobe CX Enterprise Coworker, an advanced AI teammate powered by Adobe Harness. We will showcase how Coworker and its flexible, headless capabilities seamlessly unite Adobe’s deep customer experience expertise with the power of AWS. Attendees will discover how this combined innovation enables teams to transition from manual execution to goal-based, automated workflows with built-in governance. Join us to learn how this partnership helps enterprise organizations take a massive leap forward in operational effectiveness and unlock entirely new business outcomes. This presentation is brought to you by Adobe, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"AIM282-S": {"abstract": "Your agentic applications have an accuracy and cost problem, and it's not your model choice. Sound familiar? If you're a developer trying to get LLMs to work effectively with your business context, this session is for you. Come learn how we rearchitected Elasticsearch as the database of choice for the different data types your enterprise knowledge is buried in — docs, logs, metrics, tickets, sales call transcripts, and more. From foundational innovations like Columnar and VectorDB mode with no ETL, to best-in-class retrieval that combines vector search, Jina embedding models, and native inference, Elastic is the context database for your production needs. Join this session to learn the best practices, from optimizing data preparation to retrieval patterns, to make your agents a production reality. This presentation is brought to you by Elastic, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"AIM283-S": {"abstract": "Two years ago, standing up generative AI technology was the hard part. Today, Amazon Bedrock and Amazon Bedrock AgentCore make the path to production more straightforward—but technology alone does not create value. AI initiatives often stall because ROI is undefined and business, user, and technical priorities are disconnected. Learn how Slalom’s Forward Deployed Expertise (FDX) model places a cross-functional team spanning product, business, industry, UX, and technology alongside customers. Using real data, these teams compare opportunities, prioritize the highest-value use cases, and co-build the right next thing through production, adoption, and measurable outcomes. This presentation is brought to you by Slalom, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"AIM284-S": {"abstract": "Power users in AI teams building on AWS face the challenge of frontier models delivering the best answers and token costs scaling with adoption. Not every prompt requires frontier models. The ones that do, belong on the best endpoint. This talk covers a working pattern for enterprise inference on AWS: PaletteAI Inference Launchpad running local open-weight models for routine requests, Amazon Bedrock as the burst target to extend capacity, and frontier models as needed for complex work. We will show how routing, quotas, and per-team attribution keep tokenomics predictable, so platform teams can enable AI adoption without inheriting the bill. This presentation is brought to you by Spectro Cloud, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"AIM285-S": {"abstract": "Cyber conflict has entered a new phase, accelerated by AI that increases the volume, velocity, and sophistication of attacks. The shift is not that attackers are unstoppable — it is that they operate on a faster clock than the systems meant to contain them. Their advantage is freshness: exploiting what changed before defenders reconcile it, exploiting uncertainty before it becomes understanding, and exploiting stale proof before records drift from reality. AI compresses discovery and action, scaling tasks that once required teams. In this session, learn how defense can be rebuilt around real‑time truth, continuous visibility, and inspectable, defensible action. This presentation is brought to you by Booz Allen, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"AIM286-S": {"abstract": "You don't need another post-mortem to know why enterprise AI projects fail. Organizations that have moved AI from pilot to production know it runs on context. A knowledge layer shows AI how facts relate, what's changed, and what's already been decided. Trained on siloed data, a model sees disconnected records, not relationships, and can't reason well without that context. The knowledge layer connects GenAI and agentic systems to data that's organized and accessible. In this session, we'll cover why the knowledge layer is the missing piece your enterprise AI needs, and what your organization gains from implementing one: Trust every AI answer, back to your source data. Prove every AI decision with a complete audit trail. Synthesize intelligence across disparate systems and teams. Use your existing data investments instead of replacing them. This presentation is brought to you by Neo4j, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"AIM287-S": {"abstract": "Organizations are deploying copilots, AI agents, and LLM-driven workflows faster than governance and security can adapt. Non-deterministic AI creates a new attack surface across prompts, agents, data, and runtime behavior, and acts as both defensive force multiplier and adversarial accelerant. Static guardrails are necessary but insufficient: they miss novel behavior, unsafe tool use, and risk that only surfaces when AI interacts with identities and downstream systems. This session presents a defense-in-depth model for securing AI at runtime, using NIST's Secure/Defend/Thwart framing: an identity control plane for agents, least-privilege and JIT access, runtime observability, behavior detection, and autonomous investigation and containment. We'll share a blueprint for a minimum viable AI security baseline, and why behavioral oversight is the decisive layer for securing AI at scale. This presentation is brought to you by Darktrace, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"AIM288-S": {"abstract": "Running multiple coding agents in parallel can increase throughput, but only if technical leads and engineers avoid being pulled into every agent’s execution loop. Join this session to learn how Grok Bot is an orchestration layer for technical knowledge work, coordinating workflows while saving time and mitigating costs. Through live demos, see Grok Bot operate from its own VM and browser, maintain authenticated tool access, monitor usage, and provision cloud agents on demand. We’ll be joined by a customer in production as well as SpaceXAI engineers as they share the workflows they run today. This presentation is brought to you by Cursor, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"AIM289-S": {"abstract": "This 201 technical deep dive covers the AI Software Development Lifecycle featuring the Grok product family. SpaceXAI experts and customers in production will demo proven patterns, covering which parts of the SDLC to delegate to agents first, how to structure review and testing when agents write most of the code, and where the Grok API and Grok Voice fit a production workflow. This presentation is brought to you by Cursor, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"AIM290-S": {"abstract": "AI is racing across the enterprise—showing up in endpoints, apps, workflows, agents, and data paths faster than most security teams can track. But securing AI takes more than knowing where it exists; it requires understanding how data flows, where it comes from, where it goes, and which users, devices, apps, and AI endpoints interact with it along the way. In this session, we’ll explore how to map the AI attack surface and its data lineage while securing the AI endpoints where innovation actually happens. This presentation is brought to you by Zscaler, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"AIM291-S": {"abstract": "Orchestrating AI conversations across channels requires more than just connecting APIs. Learn how to build agents that understand context, respond instantly, and adapt to channel constraints. Explore Twilio Agent Connect and Amazon Bedrock integration patterns, plus the operational playbook for deploying engagement AI at scale. This presentation is brought to you by Twilio, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"AIM292-S": {"abstract": "AI isn’t just another workload—it’s introducing agents that act, connect, and make decisions at machine speed. This session reframes Zero Trust for an agent-driven world: how to govern agent-to-agent communication, constrain access to sensitive data, and reduce risk without slowing the business. This presentation is brought to you by Zscaler, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"AIM293-S": {"abstract": "As AI adoption accelerates on AWS, cloud and platform teams need to know exactly what Veeam can do to keep their data secure and their environments resilient. This session takes you on a tour of Veeam's capabilities built for the AWS developer and cloud practitioner: discovering sensitive data, monitoring AI agent activity, and closing visibility gaps before they become incidents. You'll see how security posture and backup protection work together in practice, giving teams confidence in what's protected. And when something does go wrong, precise, granular recovery means restoring exactly what changed, nothing more. This presentation is brought to you by Veeam, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"AIM294-S": {"abstract": "Delivering enterprise analytics and AI requires an architecture designed for operational flexibility not forcing complex data movement. Snowflake's interoperable lakehouse gives teams total agency to query and write to data where it lives using the right engine for every job. This session explores how organizations establish universal open metadata governance powered by Apache Polaris while preserving rich semantic business context across tools. Learn how the Snowflake AI Data Cloud simplifies data management, lowers total cost of ownership, and accelerates time to insight for every team, and agent, across your AWS cloud environment. This presentation is brought to you by Snowflake, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"AIM295-S": {"abstract": "Building an agentic enterprise empowers organizations to automate complex work and drive measurable business outcomes. True transformation happens when autonomous AI operates across systems using trusted data, flexible models, connected applications, and a unified, agentic control plane. By bringing knowledge workers and technical builders into a shared agentic environment, teams move faster without sacrificing governance. Learn how the Snowflake AI Data Cloud unifies enterprise context, model choice, and app connections into a single architecture. Discover how to turn enterprise data into reliable, autonomous action and long-term competitive advantage in your AWS cloud environment. This presentation is brought to you by Snowflake, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"AIM296-S": {"abstract": "Tag-based allocation works until the resource stops belonging to anyone. A multi-tenant EKS cluster, a shared inference endpoint, a queue five services write to: each lands on the bill once, charged to whoever provisioned it. No amount of tagging discipline fixes that, because there is nothing to tag. This session covers where tag coverage structurally breaks down, and the alternative: measuring the running system to attribute shared spend to the team, feature, and customer that caused it, then reconciling back to the bill so the allocation still foots. Includes what this looks like in production on AWS. This presentation is brought to you by DoiT, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"AIM297-S": {"abstract": "Enterprise AI falls short when agents lack live business context. See how SAP's Business AI Platform and AWS solve this: Joule Studio enables developers and business users to build and deploy custom AI agents grounded in SAP's process knowledge, while bidirectional, zero-copy integration between SAP Business Data Cloud and Amazon Athena gives AWS services secure access to trusted SAP data. Watch a live demo of building agents that reason with business context, collaborate across SAP and AWS, and act across enterprise processes with governance.​ This presentation is brought to you by SAP, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"AIM298-S": {"abstract": "AI agents can already search, reason, and call tools. What changes when they can spend money, too? This talk explores the shift from human checkout to machine-native commerce, where payments become part of an agent's reasoning loop. We will look at how agents can discover and purchase products today, then explore Stripe’s Machine Payments Protocol and a future where agents autonomously pay for APIs, compute, data, and other capabilities needed to complete a task. When software becomes the buyer, developers may need to rethink not only checkout, but also how software itself is discovered, priced, and consumed. This presentation is brought to you by Stripe, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"AIM299-S": {"abstract": "Context graphs can reduce token usage by up to 70% while maintaining accuracy. This session explores how context graphs help AI agents understand relationships, definitions, and business meaning across enterprise data. We will look at how context graphs capture knowledge that traditional data models miss, and how organizations are applying them. You will see real examples of the architecture and results involved in giving AI the context it needs to reason effectively. This presentation is brought to you by Sphinx AI, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"AIM301-R": {"abstract": "Every organization bleeds knowledge when people leave. For mission-driven teams, a single departure can erase years of context from codebases, runbooks, and integrations. In this builder session, use Kiro — AWS's AI-powered development environment — to reverse that loss. Configure steering files that encode your team's conventions, connect MCP servers to pull context from docs and APIs, and deploy subagents that generate living architecture diagrams and knowledge bases as your code evolves. Walk away with a Kiro workspace that turns any new hire into a productive contributor from day one — no more tribal knowledge walking out the door.", "language": "en"},"AIM301-R1": {"abstract": "Every organization bleeds knowledge when people leave. For mission-driven teams, a single departure can erase years of context from codebases, runbooks, and integrations. In this builder session, use Kiro — AWS's AI-powered development environment — to reverse that loss. Configure steering files that encode your team's conventions, connect MCP servers to pull context from docs and APIs, and deploy subagents that generate living architecture diagrams and knowledge bases as your code evolves. Walk away with a Kiro workspace that turns any new hire into a productive contributor from day one — no more tribal knowledge walking out the door.", "language": "en"},"AIM302-R": {"abstract": "Every AI application touching real people can fail in ways that cause harm. In this chalk talk, we walk one application through six failure modes — PII exposure, phantom citations, out-of-scope advice, unreviewed judgments, hidden bias, and unaccountable decisions — and whiteboard a defense-in-depth architecture using Amazon Bedrock Guardrails, AgentCore, Automated Reasoning, and human-in-the-loop patterns. Leave with a responsible AI stack applicable to any form factor — chatbot, agent, RAG pipeline, or model call — whether you serve donors, patients, customers, or citizens.", "language": "en"},"AIM302-R1": {"abstract": "Every AI application touching real people can fail in ways that cause harm. In this chalk talk, we walk one application through six failure modes — PII exposure, phantom citations, out-of-scope advice, unreviewed judgments, hidden bias, and unaccountable decisions — and whiteboard a defense-in-depth architecture using Amazon Bedrock Guardrails, AgentCore, Automated Reasoning, and human-in-the-loop patterns. Leave with a responsible AI stack applicable to any form factor — chatbot, agent, RAG pipeline, or model call — whether you serve donors, patients, customers, or citizens.", "language": "en"},"AIM303": {"abstract": "As agents multiply across teams, platform owners need control without becoming a bottleneck. In this session, learn best practices for building a governance foundation for your agent platform on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore. Use gateway as a central control point for all agent-to-tool traffic, enforce authorization with policies at the infrastructure layer that agents cannot bypass, and propagate identity end to end for a complete audit trail. Use the Agent Registry to catalog, discover, and approve agents and tools across the organization, and end-to-end tracing for fleet-wide visibility. Includes a demo. Leave with a governance blueprint teams can adopt.", "language": "en"},"AIM304-R": {"abstract": "World models generate real-time video by learning environment dynamics, which powers interactive simulation for gaming, robotics, and autonomous driving, but deploying these latency-sensitive pipelines is complex. In this builders session, we deploy and optimize a real-time video world model on AWS Trainium using AWS Neuron Agentic Development skills that automate compilation, profiling, and kernel optimization through natural-language interaction. See the agent identify bottlenecks, then select optimized kernels from Hugging Face or write custom kernels from scratch. You will apply these agentic tools yourself to reach optimized, real-time inference on silicon built specifically for AI.", "language": "en"},"AIM304-R1": {"abstract": "World models generate real-time video by learning environment dynamics, which powers interactive simulation for gaming, robotics, and autonomous driving, but deploying these latency-sensitive pipelines is complex. In this builders session, we deploy and optimize a real-time video world model on AWS Trainium using AWS Neuron Agentic Development skills that automate compilation, profiling, and kernel optimization through natural-language interaction. See the agent identify bottlenecks, then select optimized kernels from Hugging Face or write custom kernels from scratch. You will apply these agentic tools yourself to reach optimized, real-time inference on silicon built specifically for AI.", "language": "en"},"AIM305-R": {"abstract": "Physical AI is transforming robotics and autonomous systems with world models that enable agents to understand and interact with their environments. Training and deploying these large-scale vision-language-action (VLA) models requires complex distributed infrastructure. AWS Trainium and the AWS Neuron simplify this with PyTorch integration — apply standard distributed training techniques using familiar APIs while the software stack handles multi-device coordination, compilation, and optimization. In this hands-on session, fine-tune a transformer-based world model, implement parameter-efficient training strategies, and deploy trained agents for real-time inference. Leave with practical skills to adapt Physical AI models and deploy robotic agents at scale.", "language": "en"},"AIM305-R1": {"abstract": "Physical AI is transforming robotics and autonomous systems with world models that enable agents to understand and interact with their environments. Training and deploying these large-scale vision-language-action (VLA) models requires complex distributed infrastructure. AWS Trainium and the AWS Neuron simplify this with PyTorch integration — apply standard distributed training techniques using familiar APIs while the software stack handles multi-device coordination, compilation, and optimization. In this hands-on session, fine-tune a transformer-based world model, implement parameter-efficient training strategies, and deploy trained agents for real-time inference. Leave with practical skills to adapt Physical AI models and deploy robotic agents at scale.", "language": "en"},"AIM307": {"abstract": "Claude Code, Kiro, and Codex are impressive individually, but in an enterprise they run stateless, isolated from team context, and invisible to governance. AgentCore turns any coding agent into a production system: shared memory so the agent understands your architecture across sessions, microVM isolation so jobs run in parallel without interference, identity propagation so agents access only what they should, and full trace observability so you know what every agent did and why. This session shows what changes when you pair popular coding agents with a production platform, and how our customer deployed this across hundreds of developers.", "language": "en"},"AIM308-R": {"abstract": "Your data lives in documents, images, databases, warehouses, and relationships between entities. In this builders session, build a knowledge assistant that works across all of it on Amazon Bedrock. Use Managed Knowledge Bases with multimodal ingestion to handle PDFs and images, add natural-language querying over structured data, and wire in GraphRAG for questions that span connected entities. Then make it enterprise-ready: secure tenant isolation, concurrency-aware ingestion, and the Agentic Retrieval API for multi-hop, cited answers. Leave with a deployed stack and retrieval patterns you can reuse across your organization.", "language": "en"},"AIM308-R1": {"abstract": "Your data lives in documents, images, databases, warehouses, and relationships between entities. In this builders session, build a knowledge assistant that works across all of it on Amazon Bedrock. Use Managed Knowledge Bases with multimodal ingestion to handle PDFs and images, add natural-language querying over structured data, and wire in GraphRAG for questions that span connected entities. Then make it enterprise-ready: secure tenant isolation, concurrency-aware ingestion, and the Agentic Retrieval API for multi-hop, cited answers. Leave with a deployed stack and retrieval patterns you can reuse across your organization.", "language": "en"},"AIM310": {"abstract": "What does it take to build a chip purpose-made for AI? In this deep dive, we explore Trainium3 architecture innovations: NeuronCore innovations that delivers higher TFLOPs, hardware-accelerated quantization, softmax acceleration, near-memory compute and Mixture-of-Experts optimizations. We'll share micro-benchmark data showing how these choices translate to real performance for Mixture-of-Experts and other demanding workloads. At the system level, we examine the Trn3 UltraServer family, scaling from 32 to 144 chips via NeuronLink fabric to enables up to 4.4x more performance. Leave with a deep understanding of how to evaluate and leverage purpose-built AI Chips for your AI workloads.", "language": "en"},"AIM311-R": {"abstract": "As AI workloads scale, infrastructure failures become inevitable, causing stalled jobs, degraded throughput, and wasted accelerator capacity. In this chalk talk, we’ll show how to build resilient AI workloads on AWS Trainium with AWS EKS. We’ll whiteboard the full resiliency stack: Neuron DRA Driver for topology-aware scheduling; UltraServer Operator for provisioning UltraServer workloads; Neuron Observer for sub-second fault detection; and Device Taint for automated recovery. We’ll walk through how these patterns support fault-tolerant distributed training and large-scale inference workloads from a single accelerator to 20,000. You’ll leave with reusable operational patterns for running AI workloads reliably at any scale.", "language": "en"},"AIM311-R1": {"abstract": "As AI workloads scale, infrastructure failures become inevitable, causing stalled jobs, degraded throughput, and wasted accelerator capacity. In this chalk talk, we’ll show how to build resilient AI workloads on AWS Trainium with AWS EKS. We’ll whiteboard the full resiliency stack: Neuron DRA Driver for topology-aware scheduling; UltraServer Operator for provisioning UltraServer workloads; Neuron Observer for sub-second fault detection; and Device Taint for automated recovery. We’ll walk through how these patterns support fault-tolerant distributed training and large-scale inference workloads from a single accelerator to 20,000. You’ll leave with reusable operational patterns for running AI workloads reliably at any scale.", "language": "en"},"AIM312-R": {"abstract": "Supervised fine-tuning enables your model to follow instructions, but teaching it to reason requires reinforcement learning. RL workflows demand iterative loops that multiply compute costs — making AWS Trainium's purpose-built price performance critical. In this chalk talk, we explore what techniques like RLHF and GRPO bring to the table and how to run them on Trainium with the AWS Neuron software stack, Torchtitan, HuggingFace TRL, vLLM, and native PyTorch. We compare co-located vs. disaggregated RL architectures. You'll leave ready to implement cost-effective RL loops that train agents to reason.", "language": "en"},"AIM312-R1": {"abstract": "Supervised fine-tuning enables your model to follow instructions, but teaching it to reason requires reinforcement learning. RL workflows demand iterative loops that multiply compute costs — making AWS Trainium's purpose-built price performance critical. In this chalk talk, we explore what techniques like RLHF and GRPO bring to the table and how to run them on Trainium with the AWS Neuron software stack, Torchtitan, HuggingFace TRL, vLLM, and native PyTorch. We compare co-located vs. disaggregated RL architectures. You'll leave ready to implement cost-effective RL loops that train agents to reason.", "language": "en"},"AIM314-R": {"abstract": "When AI agents approve aid applications, triage patients, or allocate resources, failure isn't a bug — it's a harm. In this workshop, build a production governance layer for high-stakes agentic workflows using AWS Step Functions, Amazon Bedrock, and AgentCore. Implement human approval gates that don't kill velocity, audit trails that satisfy regulators, cost budgets with automatic circuit-breakers, and guardrails for consistent enforcement of policies. Test your governance stack against adversarial scenarios. Leave with a deployable framework applicable anywhere AI decisions affect people — social services, healthcare, finance, or government.", "language": "en"},"AIM314-R1": {"abstract": "When AI agents approve aid applications, triage patients, or allocate resources, failure isn't a bug — it's a harm. In this workshop, build a production governance layer for high-stakes agentic workflows using AWS Step Functions, Amazon Bedrock, and AgentCore. Implement human approval gates that don't kill velocity, audit trails that satisfy regulators, cost budgets with automatic circuit-breakers, and guardrails for consistent enforcement of policies. Test your governance stack against adversarial scenarios. Leave with a deployable framework applicable anywhere AI decisions affect people — social services, healthcare, finance, or government.", "language": "en"},"AIM316-R": {"abstract": "You shipped an agent. How do you know it works? For organizations where AI decisions affect people's lives, rigorous evaluation isn't optional — it's ethical. This chalk talk introduces a structured evaluation framework combining Amazon Bedrock model evaluation, AgentCore observability, and CloudWatch metrics. We cover building eval datasets, measuring tool-use accuracy, detecting hallucination in agentic chains, benchmarking latency and cost, and setting up automated regression testing. Leave with a repeatable eval pipeline you can plug into CI/CD — turning agent quality from subjective to measurable.", "language": "en"},"AIM316-R1": {"abstract": "You shipped an agent. How do you know it works? For organizations where AI decisions affect people's lives, rigorous evaluation isn't optional — it's ethical. This chalk talk introduces a structured evaluation framework combining Amazon Bedrock model evaluation, AgentCore observability, and CloudWatch metrics. We cover building eval datasets, measuring tool-use accuracy, detecting hallucination in agentic chains, benchmarking latency and cost, and setting up automated regression testing. Leave with a repeatable eval pipeline you can plug into CI/CD — turning agent quality from subjective to measurable.", "language": "en"},"AIM317-R": {"abstract": "New to Bedrock, or considering it for your next workload? In this hands-on workshop you go from your first API call to a working application in a single sitting. You choose a model, ground it with Managed Knowledge Bases and websearch, apply Guardrails, pick a service tier, then deploy and monitor what you built. Along the way you learn the core building blocks and how they fit together. You leave with an application you built yourself and a repeatable path your team can follow to build the next one.", "language": "en"},"AIM317-R1": {"abstract": "New to Bedrock, or considering it for your next workload? In this hands-on workshop you go from your first API call to a working application in a single sitting. You choose a model, ground it with Managed Knowledge Bases and websearch, apply Guardrails, pick a service tier, then deploy and monitor what you built. Along the way you learn the core building blocks and how they fit together. You leave with an application you built yourself and a repeatable path your team can follow to build the next one.", "language": "en"},"AIM318": {"abstract": "Training vision-language models at scale fails in predictable ways — node crashes mid-epoch, checkpoints corrupt, and GPU clusters sit idle during recovery. In this hands-on workshop, build resilient VLM training pipelines on Amazon SageMaker HyperPod that survive real-world failures. Configure automatic health monitoring and node replacement, implement fault-tolerant checkpointing strategies, and design distributed training topologies that resume without losing progress. Using open-source frameworks on multi-node GPU clusters, you'll train a VLM end-to-end while simulating and recovering from infrastructure failures. Leave with battle-tested patterns for keeping large-scale training runs alive.", "language": "en"},"AIM319": {"abstract": "As AI workloads scale, the question shifts from \"what does this cost?\" to \"is this delivering real value?\" The answer depends on your workload, architecture, and adoption maturity. We present progressive architecture patterns using agentic software development — from vibe-coding to cloud agents to self-hosted inference to custom models — with a live demo across the AWS AI Stack, including Bedrock, AgentCore, SageMaker HyperPod, and Trainium, showing how the right metrics evolve with your architecture. You'll leave with cost-per-outcome comparisons, spend controls that work at scale, and an ROI measurement framework with architecture patterns to implement across any AI workload.", "language": "en"},"AIM320-R": {"abstract": "Organizations struggle to make sense of their data when it's scattered across inconsistent formats, ad-hoc naming conventions, and incomplete records. In this workshop, you will build a scalable pipeline that uses generative AI to transform product descriptions into structured data ready for downstream analysis. Inspired by how AWS tackles life cycle assessment across its data center fleet in the AWS Sustainability Console, you will use Amazon Bedrock and other AWS services to ingest, classify, and contextualize data to deliver sustainability intelligence. You'll then deploy AI agents to orchestrate queries and execute analysis tasks, providing you patterns for any domain.", "language": "en"},"AIM320-R1": {"abstract": "Organizations struggle to make sense of their data when it's scattered across inconsistent formats, ad-hoc naming conventions, and incomplete records. In this workshop, you will build a scalable pipeline that uses generative AI to transform product descriptions into structured data ready for downstream analysis. Inspired by how AWS tackles life cycle assessment across its data center fleet in the AWS Sustainability Console, you will use Amazon Bedrock and other AWS services to ingest, classify, and contextualize data to deliver sustainability intelligence. You'll then deploy AI agents to orchestrate queries and execute analysis tasks, providing you patterns for any domain.", "language": "en"},"AIM321": {"abstract": "Not all workloads are equal in carbon impact, but how do you know which choices matter most? In this chalk talk, we pull back the curtain on how AWS measures and allocates cloud emissions, balancing scientific rigor with practical usability. Through real-world examples, you'll see how this data reveals meaningful differences across diverse AI workloads. You'll leave with an understanding of the methodology behind your carbon data and how to apply those insights to build AI workloads with a lower environmental footprint, and, often, lower cost.", "language": "en"},"AIM322-R": {"abstract": "A member is on the line, a regulation changed last week, and the answer isn't in any manual yet. Frontline staff don't need a search box — they need a guide that surfaces the next right action in the moment. In this builder session, build a self-improving frontline navigator on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Harness: ground it in your policies with a Managed Knowledge Base, keep it current with live rule changes via AgentCore Web Search, and close the loop with AgentCore Optimization so it measurably improves from every real interaction. Unlike a static knowledge base, this agent recommends actions, adapts as policies shift, and learns on the job. Whether you serve members, patients, or beneficiaries, leave with a deployed self-improving navigator and the architecture behind it.", "language": "en"},"AIM322-R1": {"abstract": "A member is on the line, a regulation changed last week, and the answer isn't in any manual yet. Frontline staff don't need a search box — they need a guide that surfaces the next right action in the moment. In this builder session, build a self-improving frontline navigator on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Harness: ground it in your policies with a Managed Knowledge Base, keep it current with live rule changes via AgentCore Web Search, and close the loop with AgentCore Optimization so it measurably improves from every real interaction. Unlike a static knowledge base, this agent recommends actions, adapts as policies shift, and learns on the job. Whether you serve members, patients, or beneficiaries, leave with a deployed self-improving navigator and the architecture behind it.", "language": "en"},"AIM323": {"abstract": "The next major leap in AI is predicting and interacting with the physical world. World models bridge digital reasoning with real-world execution, introducing new infrastructure demands for video generation, robotics, and gaming workloads at scale. We'll explore the emerging architecture of world models and why AWS Trainium, purpose-built for better price-performance, is well suited to serve them. Learn how native PyTorch support with torch.compile fuses operations into optimized device graphs, and how NKI custom kernels accelerate critical operators like attention. You'll leave equipped to take world model inference from eager prototyping to compiled deployment using familiar PyTorch workflows.", "language": "en"},"AIM324-R": {"abstract": "Agents stall at the demo stage because core enterprise systems — ERPs, legacy databases, and internal APIs — were never built for AI. In this session, we whiteboard two patterns to bridge the gap: request-driven integration via Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Gateway with access-control interceptors, and event-driven routing via Amazon EventBridge with confidence-based human-in-the-loop fallback. We trace both approaches using a legacy system with no modern API surface — wrapping its interfaces as MCP tools without modifying the original code. The patterns apply whether your blocker is a SOAP endpoint, a stored-procedure layer, or a REST API with no auth story for agents. Leave with a maturity rubric and two practical reference architectures.", "language": "en"},"AIM324-R1": {"abstract": "Agents stall at the demo stage because core enterprise systems — ERPs, legacy databases, and internal APIs — were never built for AI. In this session, we whiteboard two patterns to bridge the gap: request-driven integration via Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Gateway with access-control interceptors, and event-driven routing via Amazon EventBridge with confidence-based human-in-the-loop fallback. We trace both approaches using a legacy system with no modern API surface — wrapping its interfaces as MCP tools without modifying the original code. The patterns apply whether your blocker is a SOAP endpoint, a stored-procedure layer, or a REST API with no auth story for agents. Leave with a maturity rubric and two practical reference architectures.", "language": "en"},"AIM325": {"abstract": "AI agents doing real work, like financial advising, coding, customer operations, reason across turns, call tools, and recover when steps fail mid-conversation. Prompting won't get you there; building multi-turn RL from scratch takes weeks. We'll whiteboard how SageMaker AI's managed MTRL connects your Strands, LangChain, or CrewAI agent under 10 lines of code - handling rollouts, trajectories, and checkpointing, auto-cataloged in SageMaker catalog. We'll cover reward design, rollouts in managed gym environments, decoupled training compute keeping GPUs from idling, and smaller specialized models outperforming larger ones at lower inference cost. Bring your agent use case for mapping the path.", "language": "en"},"AIM326-R": {"abstract": "Every team moving AI to production hits the same wall: scaling frontier model inference against growing token demand, tighter latency budgets, and spiraling compute costs. This workshop tackles that problem head-on. Experience how purpose-built chips changes the inference economics equation as you deploy Frontier AI models on AWS Trainium, apply optimization techniques, and learn how the Neuron software stack integrates with familiar open-source frameworks - no rewrites required - to achieve superior tokenomics. Leave with actionable skills to run performant, cost-effective model inference at production scale on AWS Trainium.", "language": "en"},"AIM326-R1": {"abstract": "Every team moving AI to production hits the same wall: scaling frontier model inference against growing token demand, tighter latency budgets, and spiraling compute costs. This workshop tackles that problem head-on. Experience how purpose-built chips changes the inference economics equation as you deploy Frontier AI models on AWS Trainium, apply optimization techniques, and learn how the Neuron software stack integrates with familiar open-source frameworks - no rewrites required - to achieve superior tokenomics. Leave with actionable skills to run performant, cost-effective model inference at production scale on AWS Trainium.", "language": "en"},"AIM327": {"abstract": "You already run security and governance on AWS with IAM, CloudTrail, KMS, and VPC boundaries, and AI workloads on Bedrock inherit all of it. This chalk talk shows how: IAM scoping for model access, data perimeter policies, full prompt and response logging in CloudTrail, and KMS encryption at rest and in transit. There is no separate governance stack to learn and no new tools to onboard. You leave knowing how to extend the security patterns your organization already trusts to cover AI workloads on Bedrock.", "language": "en"},"AIM329-R": {"abstract": "Extracting peak performance from AI accelerators requires optimization at multiple levels — from framework configuration to individual kernels — and the complexity grows with every new workload. Agentic loops replace this manual work with autonomous profiling, diagnosis, and code generation, iterating toward performance targets without human intervention. In this session, we demonstrate this workflow on AWS Trainium, purpose-built to deliver better price-performance for AI workloads. Explore how these loops identify bottlenecks, how persistent memory prevents repeated failures, and how long-running campaigns uncover optimizations for different inference shapes. Walk away with practical patterns for agentic optimization on purpose-built silicon.", "language": "en"},"AIM329-R1": {"abstract": "Extracting peak performance from AI accelerators requires optimization at multiple levels — from framework configuration to individual kernels — and the complexity grows with every new workload. Agentic loops replace this manual work with autonomous profiling, diagnosis, and code generation, iterating toward performance targets without human intervention. In this session, we demonstrate this workflow on AWS Trainium, purpose-built to deliver better price-performance for AI workloads. Explore how these loops identify bottlenecks, how persistent memory prevents repeated failures, and how long-running campaigns uncover optimizations for different inference shapes. Walk away with practical patterns for agentic optimization on purpose-built silicon.", "language": "en"},"AIM330-R": {"abstract": "A surprise cloud bill can wipe out a nonprofit's budget, but running frontier-quality generative AI doesn't have to cost frontier prices. In this code talk, we build an architecture that gets premium model output on a fraction of the spend. Starting with a naïve workload where every request hits the same large model, we layer in routing to send requests to the lowest-cost model meeting the quality bar, caching to reduce token counts, and token budgets with kill switches that gracefully degrade. You'll leave with working code your engineers can implement, and your finance team won't panic over.", "language": "en"},"AIM330-R1": {"abstract": "A surprise cloud bill can wipe out a nonprofit's budget, but running frontier-quality generative AI doesn't have to cost frontier prices. In this code talk, we build an architecture that gets premium model output on a fraction of the spend. Starting with a naïve workload where every request hits the same large model, we layer in routing to send requests to the lowest-cost model meeting the quality bar, caching to reduce token counts, and token budgets with kill switches that gracefully degrade. You'll leave with working code your engineers can implement, and your finance team won't panic over.", "language": "en"},"AIM3300-S": {"abstract": "Agentic AI is entering production faster than enterprise authorization models can keep up, and most access decisions are made without regard to the sensitivity of the data agents can reach, turning over-permissioned agents into high-value targets. This session introduces the three lanes: the data agents touch, the identity they act as, and the actions they take, as the framework for defending agentic AI on AWS. See how Cyera's Agent Guardian extends Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Identity with data-aware authorization to enforce least privilege, monitor agent-to-data activity, and prove it to auditors. This presentation is brought to you by Cyera, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"AIM3301": {"abstract": "When an inference fleet spans thousands of interconnected NVIDIA GPUs in NVL72 racks, scheduling decisions cascade. Place a job wrong, and it fragments a capacity block, starves NVLink bandwidth, or strands GPUs behind a topology boundary. In this session, AWS and OpenAI will share scheduling and bin-packing strategies developed for production inference on Amazon EC2 UltraServers. Explore rack-aware placement that respects 72-GPU NVLink domains, approaches for mixed workload profiles (prefill vs. decode vs. batch), capacity block utilization maximization, and Kubernetes scheduling primitives that enforce topology constraints. Leave with actionable blueprints to efficiently schedule GPU inference at scale.", "language": "en"},"AIM331-R": {"abstract": "AI agents that reach across your tools via MCP are a force multiplier — but letting an agent call OAuth-protected APIs means handling credentials, identity, and blast radius. Get it wrong and you expose sensitive data. In this code talk, deploy an Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Gateway and walk through three-legged OAuth live: secure token storage via AgentCore Identity, identity propagation across agent chains, confused-deputy prevention, and guardrails on tool results. Whether you're a 5-person nonprofit team or a 5,000-person enterprise, leave with deployable IaC for your own environment.", "language": "en"},"AIM331-R1": {"abstract": "AI agents that reach across your tools via MCP are a force multiplier — but letting an agent call OAuth-protected APIs means handling credentials, identity, and blast radius. Get it wrong and you expose sensitive data. In this code talk, deploy an Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Gateway and walk through three-legged OAuth live: secure token storage via AgentCore Identity, identity propagation across agent chains, confused-deputy prevention, and guardrails on tool results. Whether you're a 5-person nonprofit team or a 5,000-person enterprise, leave with deployable IaC for your own environment.", "language": "en"},"AIM3310-R": {"abstract": "Your agent cost $0.02 per session in testing. In production it's $0.14 and climbing. Token consumption grows unpredictably as agents retry, loop, and fan out across tools, and most teams have no visibility into where the money goes. This chalk talk breaks down where agent costs actually hide and how to control them on AgentCore: per-session budgets that kill runaway loops, model routing that drops to cheaper models for simple subtasks, cost attribution at the team and workload level, prompt caching strategies, and how to measure the tradeoff between cost and quality with evaluations.", "language": "en"},"AIM3310-R1": {"abstract": "Your agent cost $0.02 per session in testing. In production it's $0.14 and climbing. Token consumption grows unpredictably as agents retry, loop, and fan out across tools, and most teams have no visibility into where the money goes. This chalk talk breaks down where agent costs actually hide and how to control them on AgentCore: per-session budgets that kill runaway loops, model routing that drops to cheaper models for simple subtasks, cost attribution at the team and workload level, prompt caching strategies, and how to measure the tradeoff between cost and quality with evaluations.", "language": "en"},"AIM3311": {"abstract": "AI spend scales fast, and you can't optimize what you can't attribute. In this code talk, build Bedrock cost visibility live: combine CloudWatch metrics and supported invocation logs for timely usage signals with Cost Explorer and CUR 2.0 for billing reporting. Add attribution by IAM principal and cost-allocation tags, then assemble a dashboard that maps spend to teams, workloads, and models while accounting for each source's freshness and granularity. Turn that visibility into action: match steady workloads to Reserved Tier, choose models for price-performance, and set budgets and alerts. Leave with working code and a repeatable, finance-ready cost view.", "language": "en"},"AIM3312": {"abstract": "Data-intensive workloads like feature engineering, large-scale ETL, and real-time analytics push CPU-based clusters to their limits. In this session, learn how Amazon EMR with NVIDIA RAPIDS Accelerator for Apache Spark runs Spark workloads up to 3.7x faster on Amazon EC2 G7 instances—with no code changes. We share benchmarking results, walk through architecture and tuning patterns, and show how finishing jobs faster lowers the cost per run, even at a higher hourly rate.", "language": "en"},"AIM3315": {"abstract": "Agentic AI turns ML infrastructure into a dynamic scheduling problem, where the goal is cost per accepted outcome. In this lightning talk, watch a self-managed agentic workflow run financial services portfolio stress testing on Amazon EC2, with telecom and media content generation as bonus patterns. EC2 Capacity Blocks for ML reserve capacity for deadline-critical stages, while EC2 Spot Instances add elastic, checkpointable capacity for parallel work. A bounded agentic planner proposes placement across NVIDIA GPUs and AWS silicon while deterministic controls enforce budget and deadline limits, keeping cost per accepted outcome low as workloads shift.", "language": "en"},"AIM332-R": {"abstract": "AI agents are increasingly making real-world decisions — approving loans, issuing refunds, offering discounts — yet most fairness testing still focuses on whether chatbots use offensive language. This gap creates real business and regulatory risk. Join this session to explore how to assess whether your agentic AI systems apply equitable treatment across chosen demographic attributes using defined fairness criteria. Through hands-on examples spanning financial services, customer support, and commerce, discover how to move from language-level toxicity checks to decision-level fairness audits. Learn to identify disparate treatment, choose appropriate fairness metrics for your use case, and implement testing frameworks that catch bias where it matters most: in the outcomes that affect people's lives.", "language": "en"},"AIM332-R1": {"abstract": "AI agents are increasingly making real-world decisions — approving loans, issuing refunds, offering discounts — yet most fairness testing still focuses on whether chatbots use offensive language. This gap creates real business and regulatory risk. Join this session to explore how to assess whether your agentic AI systems apply equitable treatment across chosen demographic attributes using defined fairness criteria. Through hands-on examples spanning financial services, customer support, and commerce, discover how to move from language-level toxicity checks to decision-level fairness audits. Learn to identify disparate treatment, choose appropriate fairness metrics for your use case, and implement testing frameworks that catch bias where it matters most: in the outcomes that affect people's lives.", "language": "en"},"AIM333-R": {"abstract": "Advisors across mission-driven organizations—financial advisors, benefits navigators, program coordinators—juggle fragmented tools, manual research, and delayed plan delivery while clients go underserved. AI agents change this equation. See how to collapse this fragmentation into configuration using the Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Harness. Learn to securely plug domain-specific skills and disparate Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers directly into the harness to generate tailored action plans in real time. Then, deploy the agent to isolated microVM sessions utilizing native memory strategies and Bedrock Guardrails. Leave with sample configurations, deployment patterns, and blueprints for building production advisors that scale.", "language": "en"},"AIM333-R1": {"abstract": "Advisors across mission-driven organizations—financial advisors, benefits navigators, program coordinators—juggle fragmented tools, manual research, and delayed plan delivery while clients go underserved. AI agents change this equation. See how to collapse this fragmentation into configuration using the Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Harness. Learn to securely plug domain-specific skills and disparate Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers directly into the harness to generate tailored action plans in real time. Then, deploy the agent to isolated microVM sessions utilizing native memory strategies and Bedrock Guardrails. Leave with sample configurations, deployment patterns, and blueprints for building production advisors that scale.", "language": "en"},"AIM334-R": {"abstract": "Get hands-on with AWS Trainium and PyTorch's TorchTitan to train models using a fully native PyTorch workflow. This workshop walks you through distributed training on Trainium—covering cluster setup, parallelism strategies, and performance optimization—all through familiar PyTorch APIs. Whether you're training LLMs or fine-tuning domain models, you'll learn how Trainium and TorchTitan simplify scaling without sacrificing native PyTorch flexibility. Leave with the skills to scale model training on Trainium without changing your PyTorch workflow.", "language": "en"},"AIM334-R1": {"abstract": "Get hands-on with AWS Trainium and PyTorch's TorchTitan to train models using a fully native PyTorch workflow. This workshop walks you through distributed training on Trainium—covering cluster setup, parallelism strategies, and performance optimization—all through familiar PyTorch APIs. Whether you're training LLMs or fine-tuning domain models, you'll learn how Trainium and TorchTitan simplify scaling without sacrificing native PyTorch flexibility. Leave with the skills to scale model training on Trainium without changing your PyTorch workflow.", "language": "en"},"AIM335-R": {"abstract": "Neurosymbolic AI pairs the language understanding of LLMs with the mathematical rigor of automated reasoning, enabling agent decisions that are provably correct, not just statistically likely. In this hands-on workshop, you'll take real-world business rules (e.g. insurance eligibility or financial compliance) and formalize them into Automated Reasoning policies that agents invoke at decision time. Starting from natural-language documents, you'll translate requirements into verifiable logic, then integrate your policies with agents built on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore, validating outputs at inference time. Leave able to build Automated Reasoning policies that guarantee correctness, where every decision is logically grounded, traceable, and auditable.", "language": "en"},"AIM335-R1": {"abstract": "Neurosymbolic AI pairs the language understanding of LLMs with the mathematical rigor of automated reasoning, enabling agent decisions that are provably correct, not just statistically likely. In this hands-on workshop, you'll take real-world business rules (e.g. insurance eligibility or financial compliance) and formalize them into Automated Reasoning policies that agents invoke at decision time. Starting from natural-language documents, you'll translate requirements into verifiable logic, then integrate your policies with agents built on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore, validating outputs at inference time. Leave able to build Automated Reasoning policies that guarantee correctness, where every decision is logically grounded, traceable, and auditable.", "language": "en"},"AIM336-R": {"abstract": "Accuracy is necessary, but it isn't enough for agentic systems. Your agents can handle most requests correctly and still fail when someone asks how a decision was made, who approved it, or how to override it. In this hands-on workshop, you inherit a multi-agent claims system seeded with responsible AI gaps. Using the Well-Architected Responsible AI governance skill in your IDE, you audit the code against responsible AI dimensions like fairness, transparency, and accountability, investigate what you find, fix it, and generate an evidence-backed report. Walk away with a repeatable audit workflow you can run on your own agentic systems.", "language": "en"},"AIM336-R1": {"abstract": "Accuracy is necessary, but it isn't enough for agentic systems. Your agents can handle most requests correctly and still fail when someone asks how a decision was made, who approved it, or how to override it. In this hands-on workshop, you inherit a multi-agent claims system seeded with responsible AI gaps. Using the Well-Architected Responsible AI governance skill in your IDE, you audit the code against responsible AI dimensions like fairness, transparency, and accountability, investigate what you find, fix it, and generate an evidence-backed report. Walk away with a repeatable audit workflow you can run on your own agentic systems.", "language": "en"},"AIM337-R": {"abstract": "We live-build a multi-agent system where specialized agents — a document searcher, a data analyst, a claims verifier, and a synthesizer — collaborate to produce evidence-backed summaries from unstructured sources. Using Bedrock's multi-model routing, each agent selects the optimal foundation model for its task. Along the way we solve hard coordination problems: message routing, shared state, deadlock avoidance, and cost control. One presenter codes; the other narrates architecture decisions. Whether you synthesize research literature, member feedback, grant requirements, or compliance updates — leave with replicable patterns for your domain.", "language": "en"},"AIM337-R1": {"abstract": "We live-build a multi-agent system where specialized agents — a document searcher, a data analyst, a claims verifier, and a synthesizer — collaborate to produce evidence-backed summaries from unstructured sources. Using Bedrock's multi-model routing, each agent selects the optimal foundation model for its task. Along the way we solve hard coordination problems: message routing, shared state, deadlock avoidance, and cost control. One presenter codes; the other narrates architecture decisions. Whether you synthesize research literature, member feedback, grant requirements, or compliance updates — leave with replicable patterns for your domain.", "language": "en"},"AIM338-R": {"abstract": "Agentic and coding workloads are multiplying token consumption, and paying frontier prices on every call adds up fast. This chalk talk shows how open weight models on Amazon Bedrock let you scale that growth affordably without giving up quality or security. We walk through multi-model strategies for agentic and code generation workloads, the unit economics of open weight versus frontier, and how Bedrock's inference engine design keeps security strong regardless of where a model came from. You leave with a practical path to cost-efficient inference at scale.", "language": "en"},"AIM338-R1": {"abstract": "Agentic and coding workloads are multiplying token consumption, and paying frontier prices on every call adds up fast. This chalk talk shows how open weight models on Amazon Bedrock let you scale that growth affordably without giving up quality or security. We walk through multi-model strategies for agentic and code generation workloads, the unit economics of open weight versus frontier, and how Bedrock's inference engine design keeps security strong regardless of where a model came from. You leave with a practical path to cost-efficient inference at scale.", "language": "en"},"AIM339": {"abstract": "Can you train an LLM on AWS Trainium, our purpose built AI chip for better price performance, using PyTorch with minimal code changes? We'll show you with nanochat, a 560M-parameter GPT you can train in about two hours on a single Trainium instance using familiar PyTorch workflows. We cover end-to-end training from data preparation through distributed configuration to model test practices with interactive chat — showing what works out of the box and what few changes are required. Walk away ready to train your own LLM on Trainium with PyTorch.", "language": "en"},"AIM340": {"abstract": "Chatbots answer questions. Your best reps remember context, pull up the right data, take action, and resolve the issue in one conversation. This session shows how to build conversational agents on AgentCore that work the same way: low-latency streaming on Runtime, multi-turn and cross-session context with Memory, Gateway-connected tools so the agent checks orders, modifies accounts, and triggers workflows mid-conversation, guardrails that keep every interaction on-policy, and human handoff when needed.", "language": "en"},"AIM341": {"abstract": "As organizations scale AI agent deployments, the gap between managed APIs and production-grade frameworks becomes a migration challenge. This talk describes a large-scale migration from a managed assistant API serving hundreds of agent projects to Strands Agents, selected for its deterministic orchestration, multi-provider portability, and open-source extensibility. The migration replaced three subsystems (orchestration, conversation persistence, and observability) while preserving all existing tool implementations. This session details the architectural decisions, backward-compatible tool migration, security constraints for production deployment, and evaluation methodology that validated quality parity. These patterns generalize to any organization migrating from managed AI APIs to open-source agent frameworks.", "language": "en"},"AIM342": {"abstract": "AI/ML teams want to move fast - experiment with models, fine-tune them on their data, and serve them in production, without stitching together separate tools at every step. In this session, see how Amazon SageMaker AI lets you build, customize, deploy, and run inference, all within one service. Learn how to match the right approach to each stage: serverless experimentation for fast iteration, fully managed environments for custom frameworks and training, persistent clusters for large-scale research, and optimized inference for real-time and batch serving in production. Walk away with a practical decision framework and a reference architecture to start simple and scale without switching platforms.", "language": "en"},"AIM343": {"abstract": "Join us to see how Amazon SageMaker HyperPod lets teams build complete training pipelines, from data prep and transformations through distributed training to seamless model deployment, all on one resilient, self-healing platform with no re-platforming required. Then, hear how Tencent Games, a game AI team (not an infra team), went from first cluster to thousand-GPU training in weeks for their VISVISE engine powering 90+ titles including PUBG Mobile. Walk away with lessons learned from real production workloads and best practices for EFA networking, FSx storage, and GPU fault tolerance.", "language": "en"},"AIM344": {"abstract": "At scale, GPU clusters fail in ways single-node debugging never taught you: NCCL timeouts that are actually storage problems, DataLoader forks that silently hang collectives, and GPUs that pass every health check yet corrupt your results. In this hands-on builders session, you diagnose these failures on NVIDIA GPU-based Amazon EC2 instances with Elastic Fabric Adapter. Read NCCL debug logs, interpret all-reduce bus bandwidth against per-instance baselines, and run open-source health checks that catch faults before your jobs do. You leave with a failure-signature runbook and a triage method distilled from operating large GPU fleets on AWS.", "language": "en"},"AIM345": {"abstract": "Disaggregated inference separates prefill and decode onto independent GPU pools, eliminating the interference that lets one long prompt stall every active generation — and letting each phase scale on its own terms. It isn't free: KV cache must cross the network, and a well-cached unified deployment can still win. The difference is your traffic. In this hands-on builders session, you run both architectures on NVIDIA GPU-based Amazon EC2 instances, move KV cache between pools over Elastic Fabric Adapter, and benchmark them against realistic agentic traffic. Learn the decision framework — input length, prefix reuse, latency SLO — and leave with runnable configurations.", "language": "en"},"AIM346-R": {"abstract": "Setting up AI infrastructure, tuning model performance, and benchmarking throughput could mean navigating brittle tooling, context rot in long sessions, irrelevant retrieval, missing guardrails for irreversible actions, bursty GPU and CPU demands, and observability gaps. In this chalk talk, explore how to set up, manage, and scale self-managed autonomous agents on AWS to address these challenges with built-in memory, safety boundaries, and audit trails. We will walk through how to quickly deploy a reference architecture on Amazon EKS, tune a 550B-parameter model for inference using disaggregated serving with NVIDIA Dynamo and vLLM, and scale with Elastic Fabric Adapter for cross-node KV cache transfer.", "language": "en"},"AIM346-R1": {"abstract": "Setting up AI infrastructure, tuning model performance, and benchmarking throughput could mean navigating brittle tooling, context rot in long sessions, irrelevant retrieval, missing guardrails for irreversible actions, bursty GPU and CPU demands, and observability gaps. In this chalk talk, explore how to set up, manage, and scale self-managed autonomous agents on AWS to address these challenges with built-in memory, safety boundaries, and audit trails. We will walk through how to quickly deploy a reference architecture on Amazon EKS, tune a 550B-parameter model for inference using disaggregated serving with NVIDIA Dynamo and vLLM, and scale with Elastic Fabric Adapter for cross-node KV cache transfer.", "language": "en"},"AIM347-R": {"abstract": "Extracting useful throughput from GPU clusters is a discipline: profile kernels, tune collectives, feed the GPUs, and survive the failures that compound with scale. In this workshop, you walk the full stack on Slurm-managed NVIDIA GPU-based Amazon EC2 instances — compute, network, and storage; the ML software stack from drivers to distributed frameworks — and stand up the observability that ties them together, from GPU, EFA, and Lustre counters to training throughput and MFU. At each layer: measure, attribute, fix with the tool that sees inside. Leave with dashboards you built yourself and a goodput methodology for your training clusters.", "language": "en"},"AIM347-R1": {"abstract": "Extracting useful throughput from GPU clusters is a discipline: profile kernels, tune collectives, feed the GPUs, and survive the failures that compound with scale. In this workshop, you walk the full stack on Slurm-managed NVIDIA GPU-based Amazon EC2 instances — compute, network, and storage; the ML software stack from drivers to distributed frameworks — and stand up the observability that ties them together, from GPU, EFA, and Lustre counters to training throughput and MFU. At each layer: measure, attribute, fix with the tool that sees inside. Leave with dashboards you built yourself and a goodput methodology for your training clusters.", "language": "en"},"AIM348": {"abstract": "Join us in working through the ModelOps lifecycle, starting with experiment, train, register, approve, to deploy, anchored by the Amazon SageMaker catalog system of record. Using SageMaker managed MLflow and the catalog within SageMaker AI Studio, you'll build governance in as you go with no end-of-cycle compliance scramble. Training runs auto-catalog with model cards, lineage, and audit trails; you'll discover assets with semantic search, trace lineage with OpenLineage, configure access control, gate promotion on quality and compliance, and publish models across accounts. You'll leave able to trace any endpoint to its dataset, experiment, and approval in seconds.", "language": "en"},"AIM349-R": {"abstract": "Every product team wants an embedded AI assistant—but closed APIs erode margins and limit customization. In this Chalk Talk, we architect a production-ready solution using open building blocks: retrieval, reranking, and generation models working together in real time. We diagram how to fine-tune for your domain, orchestrate multi-model pipelines, and serve thousands of concurrent users cost-efficiently. Walk away understanding how Native Hugging Face models and libraries on AWS Trainium let you own your AI stack and scale.", "language": "en"},"AIM350": {"abstract": "Learn about the ways in which customers can ground their AI applications in current web information with Amazon Bedrock and Amazon Bedrock AgentCore", "language": "en"},"AIM351-R": {"abstract": "Stand up a continuous evaluation pipeline for your generative AI applications. In this hands-on workshop, you'll build an end-to-end evals infrastructure using Amazon Bedrock and open-source tooling. We cover designing evaluation datasets, implementing automated scoring (LLM-as-judge and programmatic metrics), tracking model performance over time, and triggering alerts on quality regressions. Leave with a working pipeline you can adapt for your organization's models and prompts.", "language": "en"},"AIM351-R1": {"abstract": "Stand up a continuous evaluation pipeline for your generative AI applications. In this hands-on workshop, you'll build an end-to-end evals infrastructure using Amazon Bedrock and open-source tooling. We cover designing evaluation datasets, implementing automated scoring (LLM-as-judge and programmatic metrics), tracking model performance over time, and triggering alerts on quality regressions. Leave with a working pipeline you can adapt for your organization's models and prompts.", "language": "en"},"AIM352": {"abstract": "Build and deploy a voice agent with Amazon Nova 2 SonicLearn how to build and deploy a voice agent using Nova 2 Sonic, Strands BidiAgent, AgentCore, and customized Skills. The session covers both bidirectional streaming architecture (Nova Sonic) and cascading architectures using Amazon Transcribe and Amazon Polly. Attendees will learn reference architectures, implementation patterns, deployment steps, and best practices for building scalable, production-ready voice agents using AI coding tools.", "language": "en"},"AIM353": {"abstract": "Organizations struggle to assess contract compliance as terms live in unstructured documents while structured financial and risk data sit in operational databases across the enterprise. In this session, build an AI agent that performs real-time contract compliance analysis, bridging unstructured content stored in Box with operational structured data on AWS. Using Box MCP in AWS Marketplace, Amazon Bedrock AgentCore, Amazon DynamoDB, and Amazon Aurora, you implement a multi-MCP architecture, routing to Box for contract extraction and to AWS databases for operational data, producing auditable compliance determinations citing both unstructured content and structured data. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"AIM354": {"abstract": "Multimodal AI agents need to understand more than text and interact through more than a screen. In this interactive chalk talk, we’ll explore how Amazon Nova Multimodal Embeddings connects knowledge across text, documents, images, video, and audio, including semantic search over spoken content and sound, while voice AI enables natural conversational interaction. We’ll examine cross-modal retrieval, audio and video segmentation, multimodal context, conversational orchestration, latency, and grounding tradeoffs for building responsive agents that can listen, understand, retrieve, and converse across modalities.", "language": "en"},"AIM355-R": {"abstract": "Learn how to use Amazon Rekognition Face Liveness to help confirm a real person is present during digital onboarding. This session explores how to integrate liveness checks into your application, detect spoofing attempts, and support identity verification workflows.", "language": "en"},"AIM355-R1": {"abstract": "Learn how to use Amazon Rekognition Face Liveness to help confirm a real person is present during digital onboarding. This session explores how to integrate liveness checks into your application, detect spoofing attempts, and support identity verification workflows.", "language": "en"},"AIM356": {"abstract": "We'll use Amazon Polly and Amazon Transcribe to add natural speech and accurate transcription to your applications. With Polly, you'll build expressive, emotionally engaged voices using its generative and long-form engines, covering streaming audio, speech marks for word-level timing, and newscaster styles. With Transcribe, you'll turn speech into text for captions, search, and analytics, handling real-time streaming and batch workloads. We'll show how these services work together to power voice agents, accessibility features, and conversational experiences, giving your applications the ability to both speak and listen naturally across a wide range of use cases and customer scenarios.", "language": "en"},"AIM357": {"abstract": "Build search and RAG that understand more than text. In this hands-on workshop, you'll use Amazon Nova Multimodal Embeddings that embeds text, documents, images, video, and audio into one semantic space for cross-modal retrieval. You'll generate embeddings across modalities, load them into a vector store, and build a working app that searches with text, an image, or an audio clip and retrieves relevant results from any modality. We'll cover indexing strategies, query routing, and accuracy tuning for production agentic RAG. Bring your laptop.", "language": "en"},"AIM358": {"abstract": "In this workshop, discover how to create an AI agent that takes a single user prompt, finds anomalies using observability and monitoring tools, and takes proper actions on your behalf. Using solutions available in AWS Marketplace, you can incorporate leading generative AI models into your agentic AI workflows while maintaining privacy leveraging stands agents. Deploy third-party products in your AWS account, ensuring that prompts, data, and model responses stay within the boundaries of your account and are not shared with model providers. This is important for business use cases where competitive or confidential information may be used with models. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"AIM360": {"abstract": "World models—AI systems that simulate and predict complex environments—are emerging with new modalities including Video, Vision, Language, Action to power physical AI, autonomous systems, and real-time video generation. From humanoid robotics to immersive entertainment, these models are transforming how machines perceive, think, and interact with the physical world. In this panel, hear from researchers and customers on how they're building and deploying world models in production, the business opportunities they unlock, and the unique technical challenges they present. Learn how AWS AI infrastructure powers these use cases at scale with speed, efficiency, and automatic fault recovery.", "language": "en"},"AIM361": {"abstract": "An off-the-shelf model may not meet the accuracy, cost, or latency requirements of your specialized business task. Across businesses from retail to financial services, organizations are customizing open-weight models to unlock higher value. In this session, learn how to customize open-weight models with Amazon SageMaker AI, including for domain-specific workflows and agents. Intuit shares how it is using SageMaker AI with features such as serverless model customization and reinforcement learning, on its proprietary data to unlock business value and cut costs.", "language": "en"},"AIM362": {"abstract": "Off the shelf models work for prototyping, but production forces a choice, which technique, which service, which tradeoffs. In this chalk talk, we work through the decision framework live. When Supervised Fine Tuning is enough, when DPO or RLAIF earns its added cost, and when RLVR or multi-turn RL is the right call, and what data each demands. We will learn how to choose across SageMaker Serverless Customization, Training Jobs, HyperPod, and Bedrock Custom Model Import based on traffic, budget, and control needs. Bring a real use case and we'll place it on the map.", "language": "en"},"AIM363-R": {"abstract": "Your AI is only as good as the data underneath it. Most teams treat data preparation and model serving as separate problems, then wonder why costs spike and quality drops at scale. This chalk talk connects the two. We whiteboard how to design governed data products that feed customization, inference on Amazon Bedrock and Amazon SageMaker AI, and agent workloads on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore. Then apply cost levers like prompt caching, distillation, and batch inference that compound when built on clean, governed data. You leave with the design decisions that let you scale AI with confidence and control.", "language": "en"},"AIM363-R1": {"abstract": "Your AI is only as good as the data underneath it. Most teams treat data preparation and model serving as separate problems, then wonder why costs spike and quality drops at scale. This chalk talk connects the two. We whiteboard how to design governed data products that feed customization, inference on Amazon Bedrock and Amazon SageMaker AI, and agent workloads on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore. Then apply cost levers like prompt caching, distillation, and batch inference that compound when built on clean, governed data. You leave with the design decisions that let you scale AI with confidence and control.", "language": "en"},"AIM364-R": {"abstract": "Organizations deploying AI face a critical decision at every step. Which service handles which job, and will those decisions hold up as you scale? This chalk talk delivers an opinionated decision framework for choosing between Amazon Bedrock and Amazon SageMaker AI, and when to combine both for unified security, governance, and models that move between services without rearchitecting. We cover model access patterns (API, custom model import, marketplace), deployment trade-offs between self-managed and fully managed inference, and cost-performance optimization at production scale.", "language": "en"},"AIM364-R1": {"abstract": "Organizations deploying AI face a critical decision at every step. Which service handles which job, and will those decisions hold up as you scale? This chalk talk delivers an opinionated decision framework for choosing between Amazon Bedrock and Amazon SageMaker AI, and when to combine both for unified security, governance, and models that move between services without rearchitecting. We cover model access patterns (API, custom model import, marketplace), deployment trade-offs between self-managed and fully managed inference, and cost-performance optimization at production scale.", "language": "en"},"AIM367": {"abstract": "Amazon runs thousands of AI agents internally, built on the same AgentCore platform available to every AWS customer. Teams across the company have replaced manual workflows with agents that act autonomously, cutting process times from weeks to hours and eliminating thousands of lines of custom orchestration code. This session brings together the Amazon builders behind these transformations to share the AgentCore capabilities that made it possible, the failure modes they hit at scale, and the design patterns that separate agents that demo well from agents that survive production. Every pattern discussed is one you can adopt today.", "language": "en"},"AIM368": {"abstract": "Every customer now expects AI agents in your software product. But shipping agents to thousands of customers means solving multi-tenancy, per-customer isolation, identity delegation, and cost attribution before you write a single line of agent logic. That is months of plumbing, not product. In this session, hear how Twilio and Typeform built agent products on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore - serving millions of end users without building custom infrastructure. Learn how they eliminated months of infrastructure work, stayed framework-neutral, and focused engineering on what their customers actually pay for — the agent experience, not the plumbing underneath.", "language": "en"},"AIM369": {"abstract": "The agents with the clearest ROI don't wait for a prompt. They wake on an event and run a process to completion. A new lead triggers a prospecting agent, a filed ticket triggers IT resolution, a spend threshold triggers a campaign adjustment. This session covers best practices for building workflow agents on AgentCore: triggering agents from event sources, running long tasks asynchronously on Runtime, connecting to systems of record through gateway, holding state with memory, adding human-in-the-loop approvals, and enforcing actions with policies the agent cannot bypass.", "language": "en"}};
  window.APP_DATA.sessions.forEach(function (s) {
    var x = m[s.session_id];
    if (x) for (var k in x) if (x[k]) s[k] = x[k];
  });
})();
